================================================================================

Overview:

The TinyCrypt Library provides an implementation for constrained devices of a 
minimal set of standard cryptography primitives. 

Please, ***SEE THE DOCUMENTATION*** folder for more information on the supported 
cryptographic primitives and the limitations of TinyCrypt library. For usage,
security and technicalities, please see the corresponding header file of each 
cryptographic primitive. 

================================================================================

Organization:

/lib: C source code of the cryptographic primitives.
/lib/include/tinycrypt: C header files of the cryptographic primitives.
/tests: Test vectors of the cryptographic primitives.
/doc: Documentation of TinyCrypt. 

================================================================================

Building:

1) In Makefile.conf set:
    - CFLAGS for compiler flags.
    - CC for compiler.
    - ENABLE_TESTS for enabling (true) or disabling (false) tests compilation.
2) In lib/Makefile select the primitives required by your project.
3) In tests/Makefile select the corresponding tests of the selected primitives.
4) make
5) run tests in tests/

================================================================================

AES-128 encryption: table-free bit-plane kernel

The AES-128 encryption path (tc_aes_encrypt, tc_aes_encrypt4 and tc_ctr_mode)
no longer indexes an S-box table with secret bytes.  SubBytes is evaluated as
fixed Boolean operations on "bit planes", and ShiftRows, MixColumns and the
round-key addition are performed in the same representation.  Key expansion
likewise uses a Boolean SubWord; the only table access left is the public
round-constant index rconst[i/Nk].  The tc_aes_key_sched_struct layout and the
return conventions of all existing functions are unchanged, and decryption is
untouched (it still uses its own implementation).

Bit-plane layout.  Four blocks (lanes) are packed into eight 64-bit planes,
one plane per state bit.  For state byte position p (0..15, in TinyCrypt's
column-major order, i.e. p = 4*column + row) and block lane l (0..3), plane
bit number (4*p + l) holds that byte's bit b (plane b = 0 is the least
significant bit).  Equivalently, each 16-bit column group holds the same byte
of the four blocks in four consecutive 4-bit fields: lane 0 in bits 0..3,
lane 1 in bits 4..7, lane 2 in bits 8..11, lane 3 in bits 12..15.  Because the
representation is defined purely by bit positions inside uint64_t values, the
result does not depend on machine byte order, and input buffers need not be
aligned.

SubBytes circuit.  For each plane value, the kernel computes the
multiplicative inverse in GF(2^8) as x^254 (a fixed sequence of squarings and
multiplications by x), which maps 0 to 0, then applies the AES affine map as a
fixed XOR of shifted planes plus the 0x63 constant.  gf_mul/gf_sqr/gf_xtime
operate on polynomial coefficient planes and reduce modulo
x^8 + x^4 + x^3 + x + 1.  MixColumns uses xtime on xored row planes, all on the
same bit planes.  The final round applies SubBytes and ShiftRows but not
MixColumns.

CTR mode.  tc_ctr_mode processes input in batches of up to four 16-byte
blocks.  A complete 64-byte batch goes through tc_aes_encrypt4; the remaining
tail goes through the same kernel with only the needed lanes active, so no
fallback to the old table implementation exists.  The counter behaviour is
unchanged: the first 12 bytes stay fixed, the low 32 bits are incremented
big-endian, and ceil(inlen/16) counter values are consumed per call.  The
final counter is left exactly as the previous implementation left it.  A
partial tail block writes only the input length; no keystream is cached
between calls.  out == in (complete overlap) is supported for single-block,
four-block and CTR calls; other partial overlaps are not supported.  All
argument checks, the zero-length failure, and the rule that a failed call
writes no output are preserved.

Side-channel review of the encryption path.  Every memory address referenced
by the kernel is either a fixed local stack slot, an input/output pointer
moved by a loop index bounded by the public block count, or the public
round-constant table in key expansion; no address depends on key or plaintext
bytes.  Branches in the kernel are loop counters over fixed bounds, the
public i%Nk test, and the compiler's stack-canary check.  There are no
table scans, no secret-indexed lookups, and no secret-dependent conditional
branches.  The kernel performs no heap allocation; all temporary state is in
fixed-size local arrays.  This claim covers the AES-128 encryption path only
(block encryption, four-block encryption and CTR).  It does not extend to
tc_aes_decrypt, which still uses its inverse S-box table, nor to any other
algorithm in the library.

The tests in tests/test_aes_bitslice.c back the design claims without timing
measurements: FIPS-197 and SP800-38A known-answer vectors; an independent
scalar reference implementation (a separate byte-oriented AES, not a copy of
the kernel) used to check four distinct blocks, all 256 values at every byte
position, single-block/batch ordering, in-place and unaligned buffers; an
instrumented copy of the library's CTR batch loop whose calls to
tc_aes_encrypt4 and to the shared kernel are counted, proving that full
batches reach the four-block entry and that tails do not fall back to an old
path; and checks that failed calls leave the output untouched.  The probe
copy is compiled from the library's own ctr_mode.c in the test translation
unit, so it exercises the real control flow.

Limitations after compiler optimization.  Constant-time behaviour is a
property of the source and of the generated code observed for the tested
target, not a guarantee for every compiler and optimization level.  A
compiler is free to transform the fixed Boolean expressions into code that
specifically recognizes the arithmetic (in particular, it may turn the
GF(2^8) multiplication into a table or a sequence whose timing varies) or to
introduce branches of its own.  Users who require this property on a
particular toolchain should inspect the generated machine code for the
target, or disable aggressive pattern-matching optimizations, rather than
relying on the C source alone.



